[49.373236, "o", "pdftotext \"data/1371_Walker v. Commonwealth_74 Va. App. 475.pdf\" - | head -150\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[49.388458, "o", "11/14/25, 10:28 PM\r\n\r\nWalker v. Commonwealth – CourtListener.com\r\n\r\nJune 1, 2023\r\n\r\nWalker v. Commonwealth\r\nSupreme Court of Virginia\r\nCitations: None known\r\nDocket Number: 220378\r\n\r\nCombined Opinion\r\n\r\nhttps://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9404931/walker-v-commonwealth/?q=walker+v.+commonwealth&type=o&order_by=score+desc&stat_Published=on\r\n\r\n1/22\r\n\r\n\f11/14/25, 10:28 PM\r\n\r\nWalker v. Commonwealth – CourtListener.com\r\n\r\nPRESENT: Goodwyn, C.J., Powell, Kelsey, McCullough, Chafin, and Mann, JJ., and Koontz,\r\nS.J.\r\nJACQUES LAMAR WALKER\r\nOPINION BY\r\nJUSTICE STEPHEN R. McCULLOUGH\r\nJune 1, 2023\r\n\r\nv. Record No. 220378\r\nCOMMONWEALTH OF VIRGINIA\r\n\r\nFROM THE COURT OF APPEALS OF VIRGINIA\r\nThis appeal presents two primary issues. First, we consider whether the Due Process\r\nClause of the United States Constitution requires a court to pre-screen eyewitness identification\r\ntestimony before the eyewitness can be permitted to make an identification of the defendant for\r\nthe first time in open court. We also examine whether the jury was properly instructed that the\r\ndefendant was subject to the second or subsequent offense provisions of Code § 18.2-53.1 for\r\nusing a firearm in the commission of robbery. With regard to the first issue, in accord with the\r\nmajority of courts that have considered this question, we conclude that the Due Process Clause\r\ndoes not require a court to pre-screen an eyewitness identification made for the first time in\r\ncourt. Second, we hold that the jury was properly instructed on the second or subsequent offense\r\nprovisions of Code § 18.2-53.1.\r\nBACKGROUND\r\nI.\r\n\r\nA WELLS FARGO BANK IS ROBBED AT GUNPOINT BY A MASKED INDIVIDUAL.\r\n\r\nOn the afternoon of May 23, 2016, a man wearing a mask, a hooded sweatshirt, and a\r\ndistinctive yellow work vest “barge[d]” into the lobby of a Wells Fargo bank branch. Edlin\r\nCottrell, one of the tellers, heard a scream, which another teller described as a “blood curdling\r\nscream.” Cottrell saw the masked man and ducked behind her desk.\r\nThe robber struck a customer and knocked him down. The robber then went behind the\r\nteller line to where the bank employees were. He pointed a gun at the tellers and told them in a\r\n“demanding,” and “rushed” voice to put money in a bag. His demeanor was “forceful.” The\r\nbag, a black bag with handles, appeared to be the kind of plastic bag used at liquor stores. The\r\nteller complied, as did other bank employees. A third teller mostly hid under her desk. Once the\r\nmoney was placed in the bag, the robber then ran out the front door. All told, the robber ran off\r\nwith over $15,000.\r\nThe mask covered the robber’s nose and mouth. His eyes and skin tone were visible.\r\nBased on skin tone, he was described as black. A teller described the robber as medium build\r\nand maybe 5’8” or 5’9”.\r\nA person in the parking lot observed the robber exiting the bank and getting into the\r\npassenger side of a white Acura automobile. Another person was driving.\r\nhttps://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9404931/walker-v-commonwealth/?q=walker+v.+commonwealth&type=o&order_by=score+desc&stat_Published=on\r\n\r\n2/22\r\n\r\n\f11/14/25, 10:28 PM\r\n\r\nII.\r\n\r\nWalker v. Commonwealth – CourtListener.com\r\n\r\nTHE INVESTIGATION AND WALKER’S ARREST.\r\n\r\nThe Wells Fargo bank branch that was robbed is located at Tackett’s Mill Shopping\r\nCenter in Prince William County. Approximately four hours before the robbery of the Wells\r\nFargo bank, an employee of a different bank, a branch of BB&T, located a few miles from the\r\nWells Fargo bank that was robbed, heard a report of suspicious activity. Acting on this report,\r\nshe followed a suspicious vehicle with two men in it. The vehicle was a late model white Acura.\r\nShe wrote down the license plate and reported it to the police. She also was able to observe the\r\ntwo men when they stepped out of the car. One of the men was “shorter and . . . stockier” and\r\nthe other was “taller and thinner.” The thinner, taller man was in the passenger seat. The\r\nthinner of the two was wearing a “yellow shirt.” She saw their faces. Sh"]
[49.388523, "o", "e identified Walker at\r\n\r\n2\r\ntrial as one of the men in the white Acura. Police later l"]
[49.389212, "o", "earned that an Acura with the reported\r\nlicense plate was registered to a Jacques Walker.\r\nSome of the money the Wells Fargo tellers placed in the bag was “strapped money,”\r\nmeaning a stack of bills wrapped with a band. The straps have a Wells Fargo Bank stamp on\r\nthem, which has a date and a number for a particular branch. These straps also bear the initials\r\nof a service manager and a particular teller. These straps help ensure the money is accounted for\r\nand help the tellers identify certain bundled amounts, such as $500 or $5,000.\r\nA police officer who responded to the 9-1-1 call from the bank robbery observed a\r\nyellow vest on the ground, by the road. Police collected the vest, as well as additional items of\r\nclothing, including a black ski mask and a pair of jeans, further down the road from the Wells\r\nFargo bank that was robbed.\r\nAfter the robbery, one of the bank customers, the man struck by the robber, noticed a\r\npistol on a mulch pile in the bed of his pickup truck. He called the police, who retrieved the gun.\r\nThe gun turned out to be a BB gun.\r\nTwo days after the robbery, on May 25, 2016, police arrested Walker in Maryland, after a\r\ntraffic stop. The white Acura he was traveling in was being driven 91 miles per hour in a 55\r\nmile per hour zone. The license plate matched the license plate for the white Acura that the\r\nBB&T bank employee had noted. Walker was the passenger in the vehicle. Walker had about\r\n$2,600 in cash in his pocket. There was also a suitcase in the back seat. It contained a backpack,\r\nand inside that backpack was a black plastic bag with $9,060 in cash. The cash from the suitcase\r\nwas wrapped in the distinctive bands from the Wells Fargo bank. Walker told the police in\r\nhttps://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9404931/walker-v-commonwealth/?q=walker+v.+commonwealth&type=o&order_by=score+desc&stat_Published=on\r\n\r\n3/22\r\n\r\n\f11/14/25, 10:28 PM\r\n\r\nWalker v. Commonwealth – CourtListener.com\r\n\r\nMaryland that they were headed to a casino. Police also recovered two cell phones.\r\n\r\n3\r\nWhen questioned by the police about the Wells Fargo robbery, Walker stated that he was\r\nworking for an employment service called Labor Ready that day, on a moving job in Warrenton.\r\nHe said he worked from 8:00 a.m. to 3:00 p.m. A work ticket from Labor Ready, however,\r\nshowed that Walker had worked four hours that day. The evidence established that four hours is\r\nthe minimum amount of pay a worker receives; the actual hours worked could have been less.\r\nA manager for Labor Ready confirmed that Walker worked on May 23, 2016, for a\r\nmoving job. She identified the yellow vest worn by the robber as “the same type” of vest the\r\ncompany provides to persons who work for them. She identified Walker as the bank robber from\r\na still photo. When asked how she could identify Walker as the robber, when the robber was\r\nwearing a mask, she responded:\r\nWell, he started working for us in the colder months so a lot of the\r\nworkers come in very bundled up so you learn to recognize them when they’re\r\ncoming in.\r\nSo and I had worked with him for four months, he’d been coming in every\r\nday just about asking for work so after a while you really get to know your\r\nworkers and so I recognized him.\r\nApp. 236.\r\nA search of the phones recovered from the Acura in Maryland revealed that someone\r\nused one of the phones to pull up an article titled A Man in a Yellow Vest Robs Tackett’s Mill\r\nWells Fargo. A forensic analysis of the search history of the phone revealed a query for “do\r\nsweat have DNA.”\r\nDNA testing on the mask recovered by the side of the road revealed a mixture. Walker\r\ncould not be eliminated as the major contributor to the mixture. No DNA was recovered from\r\nthe yellow vest found by the road. The pair of jeans found near the road also contained a DNA\r\nmixture that was a match for Walker.\r\n\r\n4\r\nWalker offered alibi evidence from a “good friend” and five-time convicted felon, who\r\ntestified that Walker worked on the moving job all day, from around noon to about 6:00 p.m.\r\nWalker himself testified to that effect. How"]
[49.38929, "o", "ever, this evidence differed in a number of ways\r\nfrom the alib"]
[49.391274, "o", "i he provided to a detective just days after the robbery. Walker also offered\r\nevidence from another convicted felon that a man named “Mike” may have committed the\r\n"]
[49.394427, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@78f1db70b96f:/app# "]
[98.650574, "i", "cat > /app/output/answer.txt << 'EOF'\nLEGAL MEMORANDUM\n\nTO:     Client\nFROM:   Legal Counsel\nDATE:   [Date]\nRE:     Jim's Potential Abduction Charges - Analysis and Likelihood of Conviction\n\nI. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\nJim faces potential abduction charges under Virginia Code § 18.2-47(A) in connection with his actions toward bank customer Bobby and bank tellers during the May 23, 2025 Wells Fargo Bank robbery. Based on Virginia case law, particularly the \"incidental restraint\" doctrine established in Brown v. Commonwealth and Hoyt v. Commonwealth, Jim's likelihood of conviction on separate abduction charges is LOW to MODERATE. The restraints imposed appear largely incidental to the robbery, though Bobby's situation presents the strongest case for the Commonwealth.\n\nII. LEGAL FRAMEWORK\n\nA. Statutory Elements of Abduction (Va. Code § 18.2-47(A))\n\nVirginia Code § 18.2-47(A) defines abduction as occurring when any person:\n\n1. By force, intimidation or deception, and\n2. Without legal justification or excuse,\n3. Seizes, takes, transports, detains or secretes another person\n4. With the intent to deprive such other person of personal liberty or to withhold or conceal him from any person, authority or institution lawfully entitled to his charge\n\nAbduction is punishable as a Class 5 felony when no other punishment is prescribed.\n\nB. The \"Incidental Restraint\" Doctrine\n\nVirginia courts have established that abduction charges cannot be based on restraints that are merely incidental to other crimes. In Brown v. Commonwealth, 230 Va. 310, 337 S.E.2d 711 (1985), the Virginia Supreme Court held that \"one accused of abduction by detention and another crime involving restraint of the victim, both growing out of a continuing course of conduct, is subject upon conviction to separate penalties for separate offenses only when the detention committed in the act of abduction is separate and apart from, and not merely incidental to, the restraint employed in the commission of the other crime.\"\n\nThis principle was reinforced in Hoyt v. Commonwealth, 44 Va. App. 489, 605 S.E.2d 755 (2004), where the Court of Appeals reversed an abduction conviction arising from a gas station robbery, finding that directing victims to lie on the floor during a five-minute robbery was incidental to the robbery itself.\n\nIII. ANALYSIS OF POTENTIAL CHARGES\n\nA. Abduction Charge Related to Bobby\n\n1. Elements Analysis:\n   \n   a) Force/Intimidation: CLEARLY ESTABLISHED\n   Jim used a gun and struck Bobby across the face, demonstrating both force and intimidation.\n   \n   b) Seizure/Detention: ESTABLISHED\n   Jim commanded Bobby to \"lay on the floor\" and Bobby remained there \"fearful\" with his \"head on the ground\" until Jim left the bank.\n   \n   c) Intent to Deprive of Personal Liberty: LIKELY ESTABLISHED\n   Jim's actions in striking Bobby, commanding him to the floor, and taking his personal property suggest intent to control his movement and liberty.\n   \n   d) Without Legal Justification: CLEARLY ESTABLISHED\n   No legal justification exists for Jim's actions.\n\n2. Incidental Restraint Analysis:\n\nBobby's situation presents the Commonwealth's strongest case because:\n- Jim specifically targeted Bobby as an individual victim separate from the bank robbery\n- Jim struck Bobby and robbed him of personal items (wallet and watch)\n- The restraint continued throughout the robbery duration\n- Bobby was removed from the teller station, suggesting movement beyond what was necessary for the bank robbery\n\nHowever, defense arguments include:\n- The detention was brief (approximately 5 minutes total)\n- Bobby was restrained primarily to prevent interference with the ongoing robbery\n- No separate transportation or extended detention occurred\n- The restraint was contemporaneous with and in furtherance of the robbery\n\n3. Likelihood of Conviction: MODERATE (60-70%)\n\nThe individual targeting of Bobby and theft of his personal property distinguishes this from the typical \"incidental restraint\" scenario. However, the brief duration and connection to the overall robbery provide viable defense arguments.\n\n"]
[98.650773, "i", "B. Abduction Charges Related to Bank Tellers\n\n1. Elements Analysis:\n   \n   a) Force/Intimidation: ESTABLISHED\n   Jim pointed guns at tellers and used a \"demanding,\" \"forceful\" demeanor.\n   \n   b) Seizure/Detention: QUESTIONABLE\n   While Jim commanded Janet to \"lay face down on the floor,\" the other tellers' movements appear less constrained.\n   \n   c) Intent to Deprive of Personal Liberty: WEAK\n   The primary intent appears focused on obtaining money rather than controlling the tellers' liberty.\n\n2. Incidental Restraint Analysis:\n\nThe tellers' situation closely parallels Hoyt v. Commonwealth:\n- Brief duration (approximately 5 minutes)\n- Restraints directly related to facilitating the robbery\n- No movement or transportation of victims\n- Commands to lie down were for the purpose of preventing interference with money collection\n- Similar to gas station robbery in Hoyt where court found restraints incidental\n\nAdditionally, Walker v. Commonwealth (2023) involved virtually identical facts - a Wells Fargo bank robbery where the robber \"struck a customer and knocked him down\" and \"pointed a gun at the tellers\" - yet only robbery and firearm charges were pursued, not abduction.\n\n3. Likelihood of Conviction: LOW (20-30%)\n\nThe restraints of bank tellers appear clearly incidental to the robbery under established Virginia precedent.\n\nIV. STRATEGIC CONSIDERATIONS\n\nA. Prosecutorial Strategy\nThe Commonwealth will likely argue that:\n- Jim's actions toward Bobby constitute a separate criminal episode\n- The use of force (striking Bobby) elevates the restraint beyond mere robbery\n- The theft of Bobby's personal property shows intent beyond bank robbery\n\nB. Defense Strategy\nDefense should emphasize:\n- Brief duration of all restraints\n- Restraints were in furtherance of robbery, not separate criminal intent\n- Hoyt precedent regarding incidental restraints during robberies\n- Walker case showing similar facts resulted in only robbery charges\n\nV. CONCLUSION\n\nWhile Jim clearly committed robbery and related firearms offenses, separate abduction charges face significant legal hurdles under Virginia's \"incidental restraint\" doctrine. The restraint of bank tellers appears clearly incidental to the robbery and unlikely to result in conviction. Bobby's situation presents a closer case due to the individual targeting and personal robbery, but the brief duration and connection to the overall criminal episode provide strong defense arguments.\n\nThe Commonwealth would be well-advised to focus on the substantial robbery and firearms charges rather than pursuing potentially vulnerable abduction counts that may be dismissed under the incidental restraint doctrine.\n\nRecommendation: Prepare vigorous motion to strike abduction charges based on Brown and Hoyt precedents, with particular emphasis on the Walker case's similar factual pattern resulting in only robbery charges.\nEOF\n"]
[98.651302, "o", "cat > /app/output/answer.txt << 'EOF'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
